course: dictionary
lesson: 10514
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Context, the words around the word, tells you which meaning the writer wants. The word twit is a good example. Its main meaning is a silly or foolish person, and in British talk «you twit!» is a mild, jokey insult.
explain: Now look at this: «The twit-twoo of a British wood is usually two tawny owls.» Here twit-twoo is not an insult at all. It is the sound the owls make. The words around it, wood and owls, are the clues that show which meaning fits.
explain: Capital letters are clues too. In Roald Dahl's book «The Twits», Mr and Mrs Twit are a nasty, dirty couple who play tricks on each other. Here Twit is a name, not a silly person. Always check: does your meaning fit the sentence?
fact: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context shows which one is used.
fact: Twit (noun) means a silly or foolish person. It is informal and mildly rude.
fact: «You silly twit» is a jokey insult, much milder than a swear word.
fact: Twit can also be an old-fashioned, rare verb meaning to tease someone.
fact: The twit-twoo is usually two tawny owls: one calls «ke-wick» and the other answers «hoo-hoo-oo».
exercise: «The twit-twoo of a British wood is usually two tawny owls.» In this sentence, what is a twit-twoo? | A kind of wood | A silly person | *A sound made by owls | A tease
exercise: Leo's brother shouts, «You twit, you dropped my biscuit!» What does twit mean here? | A sound in the wood | A tawny owl | A famous book | *A silly or foolish person
exercise: «The Twits» is a book by Roald Dahl. Why does Twit start with a capital letter? | Because it is a sound | *Because it is the name of Mr and Mrs Twit | Because it starts a new sentence about owls | Because it is an insult you must shout
test: «He began to twit his sister about her new hat.» In this old-fashioned sense, what does twit mean? | To sing loudly | To run away | *To tease someone | To fall asleep
test: Mum says, «Don't be such a twit!» Which feeling is the word most likely to show? | A loud cheer for the owls | A sad goodbye | *A mild, jokey telling-off | A deep, serious danger
exam: How can you work out which meaning of twit a writer means? | By skipping the word and never coming back to it | By always choosing the first meaning you see in the dictionary | By counting the letters in the word | *By reading the words around it and checking which meaning fits
